21:- [triggers-run-as-every-writer](#triggers-run-as-every-writer) — a new trigger on a mirrored table runs as every role that writes it (tally_void writes ledger rows and may not read payments); a patch's own battery that never takes that path stays green
30:- [check-passes-null](#check-passes-null) — A CHECK accepts a NULL result, so `reason ~ '[[:alnum:]]'` admits a NULL reason; name the NULL leg (`reason IS NOT NULL AND ...`); NaN likewise equals its own `round()` in numeric
31:- [early-return-branches-skip-fences](#early-return-branches-skip-fences) — A trigger's status-transition branches that RETURN NEW early skip every fence below them; fence derived columns first and compare the whole row
40:- [history-stamped-at-xact-start](#history-stamped-at-xact-start) — a history read latest-first by a now() stamp goes permanently wrong when the earlier-begun writer commits last; refuse that writer (40001), don't switch to clock_timestamp()
41:- [as-of-now-is-not-committed](#as-of-now-is-not-committed) — an as-of read at `now()` is not a plain read: it compares the caller's transaction start with other writers', so a long transaction misses a committed void; read at `infinity` for what is committed. A settled as-known answer can be one that was never true
48:- [constraint-trigger-name-order](#constraint-trigger-name-order) — a deferred constraint trigger that reads what another AFTER trigger writes must sort after it by name: once a battery has run SET CONSTRAINTS ALL IMMEDIATE it fires at statement end, in name order, before the writer
50:- [race-catch-names-its-damage](#race-catch-names-its-damage) — a race leg's bare FAIL is also what a timeout or a lost connection prints: a mutation's catch names the damage read back, the SQLSTATE or the wrong answer, with a selftest on literal outputs
58:- [every-branch-and-stated-default-tested](#every-branch-and-stated-default-tested) — a guard's exempt branch and any behaviour a header states need a fixture that takes them; -23 r2's refund exemption and K2 default had none
