/Users/ryanscomputer/code/gas-billing-memory/application/configurable-rules/wu6-domain-11-review-brief.md:93:**D9-1 cannot be implemented as written.** Your ruling — no-photo-evidence disconnect sites get supervisor-role approval, recorded as an event with a reason code, framed as "process control + audit trail, no new mechanism" — is right about the concept and optimistic about the substrate. `meter_photos.photo_type` has ten values and **no `disconnect` and no `reconnect`**. `meter_photos` has no `service_order_id`. `service_orders` has no approval columns, though `adhoc_charges` carries exactly that pattern (`requires_approval`, `approval_threshold_at_creation`, `approved_by`, `approved_at`) one table over. `service_orders` has **no event log at all**. And the approval cannot be role-checked because the RBAC model is still open — `assigned_to` is free text, not an FK to `users`, so "who disconnected this premise" has no auditable answer. The enum values and the FK are trivial; the approval follows an established pattern; **the role model is the real dependency**.
/Users/ryanscomputer/code/gas-billing-memory/application/configurable-rules/wu6-domain-11-review-brief.md:95:**`service_orders` is one of only two major lifecycle tables with no event log**, and it is the one whose output is a physical act on a household. `invoices` has `invoice_events`, collections has `dunning_events`, unclaimed property has `escheatment_events`. `service_orders` has `completion_notes` — free text — so the re-check result, the supervisor approval, the escalation, the access failure and the on-site payment all collapse into it. (`customers` is the other table without one, and A-21 already queues `account_state_events` for it.) A `service_order_events` table is a small build against three working precedents, and it is where D9-1's approval event belongs.
