/Users/ryanscomputer/code/gas-billing-memory/application/wiki-ingestion-pending.md:574:| S8 | `application/configurable-rules/workflows/disconnect-order-creation-and-dispatch.md` | NEW — takes `service_orders`' own framing seriously ("**Billing-system records of field events. NOT a field service system**") and locates the gap at the boundary: an order pushed to ServiceTitan or FieldEdge carries an address and an instruction and **not** the authorization, the expiry, or the obligation to re-check. **Findings**: the order references no determination and has no validity window (S4's finding at the operational level), with the fix split into a determination record (cheap) and a **mandatory re-evaluation the field workflow cannot skip** (the half that matters — a stamped-but-unchecked determination is still one someone relies on); **recall is one-directional in practice** while CI-082 requires it to be absolute, making acknowledged cancellation a **vendor-selection criterion** and the fallback-when-unconfirmable a written procedure rather than an improvisation; `assigned_to` is **free text**, not a FK to `users`, so D9-1's supervisor rule has no role to check and "who disconnected this premise" has no auditable answer; and nothing prevents **two open disconnect orders for one premise** — a partial unique index closes it, and two technicians at one meter is a safety concern, not just a cost. | New wiki page. |
/Users/ryanscomputer/code/gas-billing-memory/application/wiki-ingestion-pending.md:575:| S9 | `application/configurable-rules/workflows/disconnect-field-execution.md` | NEW — the last workflow before the physical act, carrying two obligations the pipeline cannot discharge for it: **re-verification** (the technician's presence is the only moment platform-answer and world-state are guaranteed compared) and **evidence** (challenged months later, the questions are always notice, protection, actual shut-off, and premise safety). **Findings**: **D9-1 cannot be implemented** — `meter_photos.photo_type` has ten values with **no `disconnect` and no `reconnect`**, `meter_photos` has no `service_order_id`, `service_orders` has no approval columns (though `adhoc_charges` carries exactly that pattern one table over) and **no event log at all**, and the approval cannot be role-checked because `rbac-model` is open; **`service_orders` is one of only two major lifecycle tables without an event log** (`customers` is the other, and A-21 already queues `account_state_events` for it) — `invoices` has `invoice_events`, collections has `dunning_events`, unclaimed property has `escheatment_events`, and the one workflow whose output is a physical act on a household has `completion_notes`, free text; **nothing detects consumption on a disconnected premise**, though `anomalies.anomaly_type` already carries `tamper_detected`, `unbilled_usage`, and `unbilled_service` — the vocabulary exists and the *rule* connecting disconnected-state to expected-zero does not, and it is the only mechanism that would surface either a failed shut-off or theft of service; and the on-site-payment window is shorter than the re-check interval, requiring a **final check immediately before the valve action** rather than on arrival. | New wiki page. |
/Users/ryanscomputer/code/gas-billing-memory/application/wiki-ingestion-pending.md:583:4. **D9-1 cannot be implemented** (S9, S8). No `disconnect`/`reconnect` photo type, no `service_order_id` on `meter_photos`, no approval record on `service_orders`, no service-order event log, and no role model — `assigned_to` is free text. Kyle's "process control, no new mechanism" framing is right about the concept and optimistic about the substrate; the enum values and FK are trivial, the approval follows an established pattern, and the role model is the real dependency.
/Users/ryanscomputer/code/gas-billing-memory/application/wiki-ingestion-pending.md:1088:**Two new Kyle decision records, committed by Kyle (`b6736ae`, `d81d34f`) — canonical rulings; ingest as decision pages.** `kyle-decisions-2026-09-22-a2-straddle-and-meter-test-anchor.md`: **R-32** an adverse billing period straddling the window is forfeited whole (the trim mechanism is removed); **R-33** meter-error corrections are adjustments on subsequent bills, not void-and-rebill; **R-34** "the last test" is the most recent completed test before the discovery test, whatever its outcome — so the meter test history (CI-091) lands first as v5.4.2-12 and A-2 becomes -13; **R-35** migration accepts partial or empty test history and never blocks onboarding; **R-36** a date-only migrated test anchors a window, marked, behind a supervisor gate that lapses six months after cutover. §7.45(7)(B) re-fetched. New finding F-1: meter-error corrections are non-disconnectable (except tampering). `kyle-decisions-2026-09-23-a2-override-and-cause-freeze.md`: **R-37** no under-reach override in the customer-favourable direction — a computed deployment bound, meter-scoped targeting, a date-validated two-code hold and an optional adverse-side tenant limit replace it; **R-38** cause and anchor freeze at evidence-freeze, posting is the absolute lock, with supersession and a tamper gate; **R-39** an eight-value cause domain, no `other`. Erratum E-1 to the 09-22 record. **Open: OQ-1** — does R-33's adjustment path extend to non-registering and tampering causes, and where does each cause's correction travel (must be ruled before A-2's adjustment-path DDL); three counsel-bundle additions. Side finding for Ryan: `meters.num_dials` vs `dial_count` duplicate the same constant. Status lines on the question records, the Kyle brief, the CI-091 brief, the Kyle queue, the parity plan, the invariant register (A-2) and CONTEXT were updated to match (2026-09-23).
