/Users/ryanscomputer/code/gas-billing-memory/application/configurable-rules/workflows/disconnect-field-execution.md:86:1. **D9-1 cannot be implemented — the photo has no type, no link to the order, and the supervisor has no role.** Kyle's ruling is precise: a disconnect site with no photo evidence requires supervisor-role approval, recorded as an event with a reason code, as "process control plus audit trail, no new mechanism." Three pieces are missing and only one is a real build. `meter_photos.photo_type` has no `disconnect` or `reconnect` value — two enum values. `meter_photos` has no `service_order_id`, so evidence cannot be tied to the order it evidences — one nullable FK. And there is no supervisor-approval record anywhere: `service_orders` has no approval columns (unlike `adhoc_charges`, which carries `requires_approval`, `approval_threshold_at_creation`, `approved_by`, `approved_at` — the pattern exists one table over) and no event log of its own, unlike `invoices` (`invoice_events`) and the dunning pipeline (`dunning_events`). **The approval also cannot be checked against a role, because `rbac-model` is an open pending decision and `service_orders.assigned_to` is free text rather than a FK to `users`.** So D9-1 is currently a procedure someone follows rather than a control the system enforces, and Kyle's "no new mechanism" framing is right about the concept and optimistic about the substrate. The enum values and the FK are trivial; the approval record follows an established pattern; the role model is the real dependency.
/Users/ryanscomputer/code/gas-billing-memory/application/configurable-rules/workflows/disconnect-field-execution.md:88:2. **`service_orders` has no event log — one of only two major lifecycle tables without one, and the one whose output is a physical act.** (The 2026-08-07 draft said "the only"; `customers` has none either, and A-21 already queues an `account_state_events` table for exactly that. Corrected 2026-08-10.) `invoices` has `invoice_events` (11 event types, append-only, with a trigger rejecting future timestamps and another syncing tenant). Collections has `dunning_events` (11 types, with `previous_stage`/`new_stage`). Unclaimed property has `escheatment_events`. `service_orders` has status columns and five timestamps, which record *where* the order got to and not *what happened along the way* — so the re-check result (Alt 1), the supervisor approval (Alt 2), the escalation (Alt 3), the access failure (Alt 4), and the on-site payment (Alt 5) all collapse into `completion_notes`, a free-text field. For the one workflow in this corpus whose output is a physical act on a household, that is the weakest audit substrate of any comparable table. A `service_order_events` table following the established pattern is a small build against three working precedents, and it is where D9-1's approval event belongs.
