| 10 | Aging of a corrected bill | **Split.** The amount already owed keeps its original age. Any increase ages from the correction date with a new due date. A decrease reduces the old aged amount |
| 11 | Forfeit-the-straddling-month under a utility's own limit | **Yes**, it applies to the utility's limit too. One rule |
| 12 | Partial payment: backbill or gas first? | **Gas first.** The amounts that can ground a disconnection are paid first; the backbill is paid last |
| 13 | Brenham "also refunded if no bill is past due" | **Two routes only.** (a) After 12 consecutive months of good payment: credited to future bills, or paid by check if the customer asks. (b) At termination: the deposit pays amounts due and the rest is refunded; if it falls short, the customer owes the remainder. "No bill is past due" is a condition of route (a), consistent with §7.45(5)(F)(ii) ("not delinquent in the payment of the current bills") |
| 14 | "No more than two late penalties": window | **Rolling 12 months** |
| 15 | Stinnett extra $125 deposit after a second disconnection | **Tariff wins, so the $125 stands for Pecan Flat.** Modelled as one deposit balance per account that is topped up, not a second deposit. Disconnections counted over a rolling 12 months. Reconnection is not blocked unless the tariff says so. Where §7.45 binds: the total is checked against the one-sixth-of-annual-billings cap, and a disconnection-triggered additional deposit is refused (§7.45(5)(C)(ii) allows an additional deposit only when use runs at least twice the estimate; (5)(F)(i): "no additional deposit may be demanded unless permitted by these rules") |
| 16 | Senior deposit with no DOB on file | **$100.** The customer provides DOB to receive the $40 deposit |
| 17 | Credit left after a deposit-paid final bill is corrected down | **A deposit refund**, always paid out whatever its size |

## Kyle's words (where they shape the build)

> 7. If it's a correction for human error it's just correcting the billing even if it adds usage. It depends on when it's caught and how large the correction is. … flag this to the operator and let them choose the action catch up on next bill or void/credit and rebill. From an audit perspective it puts the control and understanding of the business in the operator's hands.

> 12. Protect the customer, RRC will look on that with favor.

> 15. Always follow the Tariff including if the City tariff is different.

## What this changes

- **Rate changes:** the split method is a required field on a rate change, not a tenant-wide default and not a bill-time refusal.
- **Gas cost adjustment:** one locked factor per billing month; no proration path.
| 6 | SCN-0045 | Returned check: rolling 12 months or whole deposit term? | **Tenant-configurable, default the same rolling 12 months as late penalties.** Tariff governs if it speaks |
| 7 | SCN-0011 | Senior pays $100, proves age later: $60 returned? | **Yes, right away, as a correction** (next-bill credit, or check on request). It is not a deposit refund, so the tariff's no-refund-while-service-continues rule does not apply |
| 8 | SCN-0046 | Unused deposit credit later requested by check | **Industry standard: once credited, it is ordinary account credit.** Any refund follows the utility's configurable credit-refund policy (e.g. minimum refund amount) |
| 9 | SCN-0046 | Bill goes past due between refund decision (Jan 5) and credit (Jan 15) | **The refund stands.** Eligibility is locked on the decision date; the credit pays the past-due bill first (one-pot rule). For a check refund, past-due amounts are settled from the deposit first, then the rest is paid |

### Backbilling

| # | Scenario | Question | Answer |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 10 | SCN-0019 | Silent city's six-month undercharge default: counted from where? Cut short by an earlier test? | **Mirror the Texas rule's structure.** Faulty meter: count back from the test that found it. Other causes: count back from the discovery date. **A passed test inside the window cuts it short.** Straddling month forfeited (batch 1 Q11). Tariff anchor governs if stated |
| 11 | SCN-0019 | Supervisor approval when the window reaches before go-live? | **Yes: supervisor approval plus the attached legacy billing record** for those months. With no legacy record, pre-go-live months cannot be charged |
| 12 | SCN-0019 | Refund at a silent city: include pre-go-live records? | **Yes, paid in two parts with the same audit control as Q11.** Platform-era portion refunded right away; pre-go-live portion refunded once the legacy record is attached and a supervisor approves. Months with no surviving record are not refunded. Do not hold the whole refund |
| 13 | SCN-0035 | §7.45(7)(B)(v)(II) "found not to be registering": test date or first noticed? Approval with no test history? | **"Found" means confirmed** by a shop test or documented field check; a meter reader's report alone is a lead, not a finding. **If the actual stoppage date is known and is less than three months back, charge only that period** (industry pattern). **No test history: supervisor approval with the estimate basis attached** |
| 14 | SCN-0036 | Large customer outside §7.45, tariff states its own rule: limitations cap? collection reach? | **Tariff governs reach, with a four-year legal backstop** (Tex. Civ. Prac. & Rem. Code §16.004). Once billed, the backbill follows the tariff's normal collection and disconnection rules, **but no portion older than four years is ever grounds for disconnection.** Payment plan offered. Added to tariff-counsel referrals |
| 15 | SCN-0027 | Keying error adding usage where §7.45 binds: which rule? | **Catch-up on the next read is ordinary billing** (no backbill limits). **A rebill borrows the strictest nearby §7.45 limits:** six-month reach and the six-month disconnection limit, with its own cause value, "keying/billing error" |
| 16 | SCN-0058 | Partial payment: disconnectable gas first or oldest bill first? | **Two tiers.** Charges that can lead to disconnection first, oldest first; then non-disconnectable charges (backbills), oldest first. Batch 1 Q12 ranks above the Oct 15 oldest-first rule where they conflict. **If the customer explicitly directs a payment to a bill, honor it**, with an operator warning when that leaves more exposed to disconnection |

### Corrections

| # | Scenario | Question | Answer |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 17 | SCN-0006 | Per-target override on a gas correction; run-level current rates | **No per-target rate override for gas.** Gas corrections always use historical rates; run-level "current rates" stays refused. A one-customer concession is a visible, approved **courtesy credit**, not a rate change. A tariff that requires current-rate corrections is a **tenant-level tariff setting** applied to every customer. Basis: Tex. Util. Code §104.004 (no unreasonable preference or prejudice within a class). The override may remain for non-gas services |


